  "body": "# Motivation\r\n\r\nGiven that some of the previously JS-heavy page elements are now transitioning into HTML-native elements (as an example, [\\<dialog\\> element](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element/dialog) replacing modal windows previously only implementable with JavaScript), it feels nice to have custom elements definable in an HTML-only way. Given that custom elements are probably the most flexible way to abstract/reuse page elements, having an HTML-only way to define these would contribute to more lightweight pages and overall simpler designs.\r\n\r\nI myself am interested in this feature because I have lots of HTML/CSS-only/mostly-HTML/CSS projects and only including `<script>` tag to define custom elements is a contribution to overall page size and an exception to the otherwise HTML/CSS-only designs.\r\n\r\n# Intuition for the API (not in any way binding, just the use I'm imagining as the best one for me)\r\n\r\nThere are most of the building blocks in the standard already: `<template>` and `<slot>` as the flexible ways to define patterns for new elements (former), and define placeholders for its content (latter). What's left is actually defining elements based on `<templates>`. If we take the [MDN tutorial on custom elements](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_components/Using_templates_and_slots#using_templates_with_web_components) as an example, we'll have this HTML code:\r\n``` html\r\n<template id=\"my-paragraph\">\r\n  <style>\r\n    p {\r\n      color: white;\r\n      background-color: #666;\r\n      padding: 5px;\r\n    }\r\n  </style>\r\n  <p><slot name=\"my-text\">My default text</slot></p>\r\n</template>\r\n```\r\n\r\nand a JS code to drive it:\r\n``` js\r\ncustomElements.define(\r\n  \"my-paragraph\",\r\n  class extends HTMLElement {\r\n    constructor() {\r\n      super();\r\n      let template = document.getElementById(\"my-paragraph\");\r\n      let templateContent = template.content;\r\n\r\n      const shadowRoot = this.attachShadow({ mode: \"open\" });\r\n      shadowRoot.appendChild(templateContent.cloneNode(true));\r\n    }\r\n  },\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\nwith the use being:\r\n``` html\r\n<my-paragraph>\r\n  <span slot=\"my-text\">Let's have some different text!</span>\r\n</my-paragraph>\r\n```\r\n\r\n-----------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n\r\nThe JS code is mostly repetitive in structure for most custom elements, which feels like something to abstract. What if we just defined custom element via `<template>` itself? Like:\r\n\r\n``` html\r\n<template id=\"my-paragraph\" custom=\"my-paragraph\">\r\n   <!-- Commented out for brevity -->\r\n  <p><slot name=\"my-text\">My default text</slot></p>\r\n</template>\r\n```\r\n\r\nNotice the `custom=\"my-paragraph\"` part—let's say it defines the custom element `my-paragraph` to expand to this template. If it was so, then the use of this custom element would be the same:\r\n``` html\r\n<my-paragraph>\r\n  <span slot=\"my-text\">Let's have some different text!</span>\r\n</my-paragraph>\r\n```\r\nBut without JavaScript!\r\n\r\nSupposed advantages:\r\n- JavaScript defining custom element would simply cease existing. Which is neat and short.\r\n- Another possible iteration on this idea would be to allow inheriting build-in elements. Say, with an `inherits=\"p\"` attribute.\r\n- And then, non-standard `shadowrootmode` attribute could also be extended in semantics to auto-generate the Shadow DOM node including this `<template>`.\r\n\r\nAgain, this is not a definitive design, I am more than ready to accept a more elegant/standard-compliant/etc. way to do the same thing!\r\n\r\n# Shortcomings of the Suggestion\r\n\r\nIn my small example I've used non-standard `custom` and `inherits` attributes and overall strange structure (why should a `<template>` define custom element? Maybe it's better defined in some other element or in some other way?), which kind of hints at the clunkiness of imagining/designing/brainstorming custom-elements-via-HTML idea, at least in my intuition for that.\r\n\r\nSuch a simplistic approach is also much less customizable than the JS-heavy custom element creation, because declarative HTML is not ever a rival to Turing-complete JavaScript.\r\n\r\nBut! I consider this HTML-native custom element definition a huge progress, because it removes the need to write boilerplate JavaScript code exactly for the cases of simple elements, and, well, use JavaScript at all. And the degree of customization it provides via slots is quite high, so it could go a long way without JavaScript!\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n\r\nI've read the contribution guide, FAQ, Working Mode, Code of Conduct, and filed the Participant Agreement. I hope that it's all compliant with the established procedures. If not, I'll be glad to be corrected or redirected to another place this issue belongs better to.",
